{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Your brother Israel — you know, etc.</b> The reason for His saying <b>your brother</b>, and also His saying <b>you know</b>, is to say that this affliction was not occasioned by me but by our fathers, and I and you are equal brothers in the matter, for this promissory note of affliction was written against our father, and one of the brothers paid it. And you will find that they of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 82) on the verse ״וַיֵּלֶךְ אֶל אֶרֶץ״ (Genesis 36:8), and this is their language: ״because of the debt, that he did not wish to pay his father's debt״ — end of quotation." "And His saying <b>you know</b> — meaning: already before the affliction befell us, you knew of it, for it was decreed between the pieces (i.e., the covenant between the pieces). And this particular thing is precisely what he knew; therefore he was precise in saying <b>you</b> — the nations thought that the occasion of the descent was mere chance, that they became slaves and lowly." "And His saying <b>and our fathers went down</b>, etc. — meaning: besides the exile, an additional doubled affliction that we suffered was that the exile was in Egypt. This is along the lines of what the Tosafot wrote in tractate Shabbat (Shabbat 10a), regarding what the Gemara said there, and this is their language: ״A person should never single out one son among the sons, for because of two sela's of fine wool, etc., the matter rolled on, and our fathers went down to Egypt״ — end of quotation. And the Tosafot raised the difficulty: but was not the decree already from between the pieces? And they resolved that if not for the change Jacob made for Joseph, the exile would have been in another place, without such intensity of servitude with which they excelled in embittering their lives. From this it follows that the exile's being in Egypt is something added beyond what was known in the decree; therefore he said <b>and they went down</b>, etc. — meaning: and furthermore, we had the affliction in this evil place for many days." "<b>and they did evil to us</b>, etc. And through this statement the intention is also to say that they paid the promissory note of debt, and from this side they acquired the land. And they also informed them that they paid a great payment, with all that much distress over a long time, as he says <b>many days</b>."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:11:28.427937703Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}